Track 1: Solder-Sealed Postcard to the Bottom of the Dock
- Story & Context: In October 1974, sound engineer Karlheinz Vorse dropped an unmailed letter to his estranged sister into a zinc casing and plunged it into the Bremerhaven harbor just as two cargo vessels sounded their horns in an accidental B-flat major dyad. The resulting shockwave stirred the harbor silt, inspiring the band to record the track's monstrous, opening brass-and-Mellotron wall that mimics the collision of metal and water. The rhythm is driven by an erratic, close-miked hi-hat pattern that ticks like a brass clock drowning in brine, setting the entire genre's blueprint in a single night of drunken studio madness. As the fog horn synths crest over the groove, the vocalist bellows through a copper megaphone: "The water accepts what the postman forgot!"
